Answer 6: We recommend Agile-would because you can change requireents even late in development. And it is designed to handle changing and evolving requirements. 
Agile divides development into short iterations or sprints. At the end of each iteration, the customer can review the product, provide feedback, and request changes. 

7-Compare Scrum, Extreme Programming (XP), and Feature-Driven Development (FDD). What are the main characteristics of each model, and when would you choose one over the others?
Answer 7: Scrum focuses primarily on managing the development process. Work is divided into short Sprints, and requirements are maintained in a prioritized Product Backlog. 
The team reviews progress regularly and adapts to changing requirements. You choose scrum when you need a simple framework for organizing a team, prioritizing changing requirements.

8-What are the main advantages and disadvantages of Scrum? Discuss situations in which Scrum may work well and situations in which it may not be suitable.
Answer 8: Scrum can handles changing requirements, frequent delivery, continous feedback and improved teamwork. But it requires experienced and disciplined teams, scope can change frequently and scaling can be difficult.
Scrum work well when requirements are uncertain , the customer needs to provide regular feedback but it may not be suitable when requirements are completely fixed and unlike to change and when the project requires a detailed plan.

